# EtsyPilot environment template.
#
# Every value here is a placeholder. Never commit real credentials, and never
# expose a secret to the client - only NEXT_PUBLIC_* reaches the browser bundle.
#
# ONE LINE IN HERE NOW NEEDS YOUR ATTENTION: AUTH_MODE is set to "live", which
# requires a Supabase project and a DATABASE_URL. Set it to "mock" and the app
# runs completely on the demo shop with nothing else configured — every screen
# works, and that is how the tests and browser checks run.
#
# Apart from that line, nothing below is required to boot or browse.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- App
# A PLACEHOLDER, not a value. Set it to wherever this deployment actually
# serves from; there is no domain yet, so there is nothing true to put here.
# Nothing in the authentication or password-reset code reads it — those take no
# host at all — so a wrong value here cannot break signing in.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_NAME=EtsyPilot

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Mode
# "mock" serves the Willow & Fern demo shop and cannot write to Etsy.
# "live" requires the Etsy credentials below and is Phase 11.
#
# This controls the ETSY ADAPTER ONLY. It does not decide whether a real person
# is signed in — see AUTH_MODE below.
ETSY_MODE=mock

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Auth mode
# "mock" (default) returns the fixed demo session: Salman R. on the Willow &
# Fern shop, no credentials of any kind required.
# "live" reads a real Supabase session and requires the Supabase values below.
#
# DELIBERATELY INDEPENDENT OF ETSY_MODE, because the state we are actually in
# needs two flags to describe: real sellers signing in while shop data is still
# the demo catalogue, because the Etsy API key has not arrived yet.
#
# One flag could not express that. Turning authentication on would have
# switched the Etsy adapter to live at the same moment and broken every screen
# that reads a listing, an order or a fee.
#
# The pairing that does NOT work is AUTH_MODE=mock with ETSY_MODE=live: live
# Etsy data attributed to a fabricated demo user. Nothing enforces that yet
# because neither live path is wired; it is written down here so the ordering
# is a decision rather than a discovery.
#
# "live" NEEDS THREE THINGS SET, all below: NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL,
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY and DATABASE_URL. Without all three, getSession()
# logs the misconfiguration and returns null, so every screen redirects to
# /login and the sign-in form cannot work. It fails loudly rather than quietly
# serving the demo shop to someone who asked for real accounts.
#
# FULLY REVERSIBLE. Set this back to "mock", or delete the line, and the app
# returns to the fixed demo session with no database and no Supabase project —
# every screen works exactly as before. The test suite and the browser checks
# all run that way.
AUTH_MODE=live

# ----------------------------------------------------------------- Etsy
# LEAVE ALL OF THESE BLANK UNTIL YOU HAVE AN ETSY APP. The product runs, builds
# and tests without them; every screen works on the demo shop. See
# docs/ETSY-SETUP.md for the full checklist when your key arrives.
#
# Server-only, all of them. None is NEXT_PUBLIC_, none is ever returned in an
# API response, and none appears in any URL the browser sees.

# Your app's keystring from https://www.etsy.com/developers/your-apps.
# Sent as the x-api-key header, server to server, and as the public client_id in
# the authorize URL — that is what Etsy's OAuth expects.
ETSY_API_KEY=

# Only needed for confidential-client flows. EtsyPilot uses PKCE, so the
# authorization code is bound to the browser that started the flow and this can
# stay empty.
ETSY_API_SECRET=

# Must match the redirect URI registered in the Etsy app dashboard character
# for character, including scheme, port and trailing path. Etsy's rejection
# message does not say which part differed.
#   local:      http://localhost:3000/api/etsy/callback
#   production: https://your-domain/api/etsy/callback
ETSY_REDIRECT_URI=

# Encrypts Etsy refresh tokens at rest (AES-256-GCM), so a database backup is
# not a place a token is readable. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
# Must be exactly 32 bytes base64-encoded; the app refuses to start a live
# connection with anything else rather than silently storing plaintext.
# Rotating this invalidates stored tokens — sellers reconnect, nothing is lost.
TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Database
# Not needed in demo mode.
DATABASE_URL=

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Supabase Auth
# Only read when AUTH_MODE=live. Blank is correct until then.
#
# Newer Supabase projects issue sb_publishable_... / sb_secret_... keys instead
# of JWT anon keys. Both work unchanged with @supabase/ssr — the key is a bearer
# credential that is forwarded, never decoded, so nothing in this codebase
# parses it as a JWT.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# Public by design: it identifies the project to a browser and is protected by
# row-level security, not by secrecy.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------- Two-factor and password reset
# NO VARIABLE TO SET HERE, and that is the point rather than an omission.
#
# Both features use Supabase Auth's own primitives, so the configuration lives
# in the Supabase dashboard and not in this file:
#
#   MFA (TOTP)          Authentication -> Sign In / Providers. Without it,
#                       enrollment fails and NO OPERATOR CAN OPEN /admin —
#                       the second factor is required with no grace period.
#   Recovery codes      A newer GoTrue feature, enabled alongside MFA where it
#                       is offered. Without it, enrollment still works and the
#                       screen says plainly that a lost phone needs a manual
#                       intervention in the dashboard.
#   Reset email         Authentication -> Emails -> Reset Password. The
#                       template must emit {{ .Token }} — the six-digit code —
#                       instead of {{ .ConfirmationURL }}. With the default
#                       template the email carries a link and the reset screen
#                       waits for digits that never arrive.
#   SMTP                Project Settings -> Auth. Supabase's built-in sender is
#                       capped at a few messages per hour and is not for
#                       production. It needs a verified sending domain, which
#                       needs a domain. Two-factor sends no email and works
#                       today regardless.
#
# NO SITE URL IS NEEDED. The code flow passes no redirectTo, so there is no
# host anywhere in the auth code and nothing to edit when a domain arrives —
# that change is an environment value and some dashboard settings, never a
# file. docs/DATABASE-AND-AUTH-SETUP.md step 2b has the full checklist.
#
# NOTHING STORES A SECOND FACTOR OR A RESET CODE IN OUR DATABASE. Supabase
# holds both, with their expiry, single-use and rate limiting. A second copy
# would be a second thing that can leak, and a readable table of reset codes is
# a password reset for every account in it.

# NEVER NEXT_PUBLIC_, never in a client component, never in an API response.
# This key BYPASSES ROW-LEVEL SECURITY — it can read and write every seller's
# rows regardless of who is signed in. Nothing in this codebase reads it today,
# and a session read never should: a session runs as the signed-in user or it
# is not a session. There is a browser check that fails if a credential pattern
# appears in .next/static.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------- Operator access
# Who may open /admin. Comma-separated email addresses.
#
# THESE TWO ARE NEVER READ FROM THE DATABASE, and that is the point. A database
# compromise must not be able to mint an administrator — writing a row is a far
# lower bar than editing a deployment's environment. It is also the break-glass
# route: if the promotion UI is broken or locked, an env var and a restart
# still gets someone in.
#
# Matching is trimmed and case-insensitive on both sides, so a stray space
# cannot silently lock out your only administrator and capitalisation never
# decides the outcome either way. An empty entry ("a@x.com,,b@x.com") is
# dropped rather than kept, so an account with no email can never match it.
#
# Unset, empty or unparseable means nobody. There is no branch that grants on
# absence.
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAILS=
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# MANAGER is not here: it lives in users.platform_role and is granted in-app,
# from Accounts → Change role. It is a PLATFORM role — who may operate
# EtsyPilot — and has nothing to do with memberships.role, which is a SHOP role
# (D20). Every seller owns their own shop; none of them is thereby an operator.
#
# ONLY A SUPER_ADMIN MAY PROMOTE OR DEMOTE, and the panel can only ever set
# MANAGER or USER — the two roles above are not offered, because a panel that
# could grant itself the top role would defeat the reason they live here. That
# is a property of the types on the write path, not a check.
#
# An account named in either list ABOVE cannot be edited from the panel at all.
# Writing its row would succeed and change nothing, since these variables
# outrank the column, so the panel says so instead of offering a control that
# does nothing. To change one of those, edit the list here and restart.
#
# A SUPER_ADMIN also edits the PERMISSION MATRIX — what admins and managers may
# each do — from Operations → Permissions. Super admin's own set is not
# editable and is always all seven, because a super admin who could remove
# their own capabilities would lock themselves out of the screen that restores
# them. The sets live in admin_role_permissions and are seeded from the
# built-in defaults, so a fresh deployment behaves exactly as it did before the
# matrix existed.
#
# Every promotion, demotion, permission change and REFUSED attempt — including
# a wrong password — is written to the audit log, which only a SUPER_ADMIN can
# read. All of them require re-entering your own password; that check is rate
# limited to 10 attempts per 15 minutes per operator, shared across both forms.
#
# A change and its audit record are ONE TRANSACTION. If the record cannot be
# written the change is rolled back and you are told so, rather than a
# privilege changing with nothing to show who changed it.
#
# /admin is unreachable entirely unless AUTH_MODE=live, because demo mode's
# session is shared by everyone who can reach the deployment.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- AI
# "mock" (default) runs the deterministic rule-based drafter. Nothing leaves
# the machine and no credentials are needed.
#
# "live" additionally requires ANTHROPIC_API_KEY. Both must be set: a mode that
# claims to be live with no key fails when a seller clicks the button rather
# than when someone misconfigured it.
#
# The key is read server-side only. It is never sent to the browser, never
# returned in an API response, and lib/ai/claude.ts is marked server-only so
# importing it from a client component is a build error.
AI_MODE=mock
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ Billing
# Server-only. Billing stays on the mock provider unless BOTH ETSY_MODE=live and
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY are set — a stray key in a developer's environment must not
# put the demo shop on a live billing path.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# Required to accept webhooks. Without it the endpoint refuses every request.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The only billing value that may reach the browser.
NEXT_PUBLIC_STRIPE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# -------------------------------------------------------- Browser extension
# Comma-separated extension ids allowed to call /api/extension/*. Any other
# origin gets no CORS headers and cannot read a response, even with a valid
# session cookie. Empty means no extension can call the app.
EXTENSION_IDS=
# Where the packaged popup is told to find this app. Baked in at build time.
EXTENSION_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ------------------------------------------------------------ Observability
# debug | info | warn | error. Defaults to info in production, debug otherwise.
# Logs are one JSON object per line, redacted at the writer — see
# lib/observability/logger.ts.
LOG_LEVEL=

# ------------------------------------------- Third parties (all off by default)
# Each of these SENDS SELLER DATA SOMEWHERE, so each is a separate switch and
# each stays off until its credential is set. There is no single TELEMETRY=on:
# wanting error reports is not consenting to analytics, and neither implies
# email. All three also refuse to run in demo mode — a stray key in a
# developer's environment must not put a demo shop on a live pipeline.
#
# Settings → Export & deletion reads these adapters at request time and tells
# the seller which are on, so that page cannot claim "nothing is sent" while
# one of them is.

# Error tracking. Errors are redacted before they leave (lib/observability).
SENTRY_DSN=

# Product analytics. Public by design — it identifies the project to a browser,
# and treating it as a secret would be theatre. What it may carry is bounded by
# ANALYTICS_EVENTS and AnalyticsProps in lib/telemetry/interface.ts: counts of
# named actions, never listing text, search terms or shop names.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=

# Transactional email only — a finished bulk job, a failed sync, a plan change.
# There is no campaign send in this product and no list to send one to.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------- Jobs
INNGEST_EVENT_KEY=
INNGEST_SIGNING_KEY=
